<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I5782036953f70847440b4c4727e42d54261148ad" commit="6073cdb1ed144677d99db440a2b48c5d522949c1" commit_message="poppy: initial commit for the baseboard/overlay  BUG=chrome-os-partner:59848 TEST=setup_board --board=poppy &amp;&amp; build_packages --board=poppy CQ-DEPEND=CL:414926,CL:*307358,CL:*307338  Change-Id: I5782036953f70847440b4c4727e42d54261148ad Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/414193 Commit-Ready: YH Lin &lt;yueherngl@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: YH Lin &lt;yueherngl@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mohammed Habibulla &lt;moch@google.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="414193" owner_email="yueherngl@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="8" project="chromiumos/overlays/board-overlays" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/board-overlays" ref="refs/changes/93/414193/8" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ia88233408af6d3e2bd08de1fa534b6507c619945" commit="1f4b07f8beec5fed081118d07691bfd070e156e4" commit_message="trunks: optional hash generation in Sign() and Verify()  TpmUtility doesn't always need to generate the hash of a plaintext in Sign() and Verify(): sometimes a ready-to-use hash of the input data is generated by the called and passed to these methods. Chaps does that, for example. To support such use cases, add a parameter to Sign() and Verify() that tells if the hash needs to be generated by TpmUtility.  CQ-DEPEND=CL:414956 BUG=chrome-os-partner:59754 TEST=1) For a pre-generated key pair (/tmp/priv.key + /tmp/pub.key) and input      /tmp/1.txt, import the private key, sign the text using it, and verify      that the signature is correct:        openssl pkcs8 -inform pem -outform der -in /tmp/priv.key \                      -out /tmp/priv.der -nocrypt        p11_replay --import --path=/tmp/priv.der --type=privkey -id=bbbbbb        pkcs11-tool --module=`ls /usr/lib**/libchaps.so` --slot=0 --id=bbbbbb \                    --sign &lt;/tmp/1.txt &gt;/tmp/1.sig        openssl dgst -sha1 -verify /tmp/pub.key -signature /tmp/1.sig /tmp/1.txt      The last operation should say &quot;Verified OK&quot;.      2) Unit tests.  Change-Id: Ia88233408af6d3e2bd08de1fa534b6507c619945 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/414278 Commit-Ready: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="414278" owner_email="apronin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="aosp/platform/system/tpm" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/aosp/platform/system/tpm" ref="refs/changes/78/414278/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ic76364aa73f3e2186fe196367d970bc5347a30af" commit="6b67c61a513e9ece309892896c67c3d56e19e7f8" commit_message="chaps, cryptohome: fix signing/verification for TPM2-backed keys  In TPM2 case, the tpm prepends the encoded algorithm ID to form the PKCS-v1.5 DigestInfo. So, use the prefix to determine the algorithm ID and then strip it from the data passed to TPM.  Also, make sure TPM doesn't hash again the already generated hash for the signed plaintext - the trunks TpmUtility Sign() and Verify() methods now accept a flag that tells if hashing should be done by these methods. Chaps passes the already generated hashes, so set this flag to false there.  CQ-DEPEND=CL:414278 BUG=chrome-os-partner:59754 TEST=1) For a pre-generated key pair (/tmp/priv.key + /tmp/pub.key) and input      /tmp/1.txt, import the private key, sign the text using it, and verify      that the signature is correct:        openssl pkcs8 -inform pem -outform der -in /tmp/priv.key \                      -out /tmp/priv.der -nocrypt        p11_replay --import --path=/tmp/priv.der --type=privkey -id=bbbbbb        pkcs11-tool --module=`ls /usr/lib**/libchaps.so` --slot=0 --id=bbbbbb \                    --sign &lt;/tmp/1.txt &gt;/tmp/1.sig        openssl dgst -sha1 -verify /tmp/pub.key -signature /tmp/1.sig /tmp/1.txt      The last operation should say &quot;Verified OK&quot;.      2) Unit tests.  Change-Id: Ic76364aa73f3e2186fe196367d970bc5347a30af Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/414956 Commit-Ready: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="414956" owner_email="apronin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="5" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/56/414956/5" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I8bae7f89d922d6ff58c720d0a41a2b6d41a5f9cd" commit="60c687bb669fe20ad54593fa1b0eded1c0406942" commit_message="shill: wimax: destroy all services upon disconnecting from WiMAX manager  When the WiMAX manager (a separate DBus daemon) vanishes from DBus, shill is notified by DBus and triggers shill::WiMaxProvider to destroy all WiMAX device objects (and their DBus adaptors). However, only the selected WiMAX service object associated with a WiMAX device object may be indirectly destroyed along with the device object.  To prevent WiMaxProvider from holding any remaining service object, which may indirectly hold a device object, this CL modifies WiMaxProvider::DisconnectFromWiMaxManager() to explicitly destroy all services held by WiMaxProvider. This ensures all WiMAX device objects and their associated DBus adaptors are released, so that when WiMaxProvider::ConnectToWiMaxManager() is invoked again, shill can recreate a WiMAX device and its associated DBus adapter with the same DBus interface and object path without a conflict.  BUG=chromium:669225 TEST=Run unit tests (with WiMAX enabled by USE='gdmwimax wimax').  Change-Id: I8bae7f89d922d6ff58c720d0a41a2b6d41a5f9cd Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/415029 Commit-Ready: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Kevin Cernekee &lt;cernekee@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="415029" owner_email="benchan@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="aosp/platform/system/connectivity/shill" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/aosp/platform/system/connectivity/shill" ref="refs/changes/29/415029/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ia0cc7f9279cb460e20a8faf332ad432067dc5482" commit="8e266f774c45480a8b3cfde94ce78807f58c568a" commit_message="charger: Add CONFIG option to maintain VBAT voltage  On the bd9995*, back boosting may occur when actual battery voltage drops below VBAT register setting. Maintain the VBAT register at the battery-requested charge voltage even when not charging to ensure the bd9995* doesn't become a back boosted animal.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:56139,chrome-os-partner:54248 BRANCH=gru TEST=Manual on kevin, unplug AC, run 'charger', verify that 'V_batt' is maintained at 8688 mV. Attach charger, verify 'V_batt' stays at 8688 mV and device charges.  Change-Id: Ia0cc7f9279cb460e20a8faf332ad432067dc5482 Signed-off-by: Shawn Nematbakhsh &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/400087 Commit-Ready: Daisuke Nojiri &lt;dnojiri@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Vijay P Hiremath &lt;vijay.p.hiremath@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Daisuke Nojiri &lt;dnojiri@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="400087" owner_email="shawnn@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/87/400087/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I147924487988481aa78e3000f55631bd9a4fdc28" commit="b487887ce18a08c1ffabc8c78409cda0f4665fe6" commit_message="power: Clean up handling of initial tablet mode.  Don't report a fake tablet mode change from within Daemon::Init(). Also add Daemon tests to verify that we're no longer doing this and that wifi transmit power is updated in response to tablet mode changes.  BUG=chromium:661368 TEST=added tests  Change-Id: I147924487988481aa78e3000f55631bd9a4fdc28 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/414593 Commit-Ready: Dan Erat &lt;derat@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Dan Erat &lt;derat@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Dan Erat &lt;derat@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="414593" owner_email="derat@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/93/414593/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.10" change_id="I4bd74e07708b9758d90878c5d4a7068369679cdf" commit="96d296dabdd0c6fa1bf1eedde73fccb6e5b18aab" commit_message="CHROMIUM: video: tegra: host: Add submit checks  Currently nvhost performs minimal checking for submits it passes to hardware: The kernel does not check if job syncpoints are allocated and the gather classes are not verified currently.  This patch adds checks for syncpoint ids and gather classes. This patch fixes a security vulnerability reported here: https://code.google.com/p/android/issues/detail?id=223929  Change-Id: I4bd74e07708b9758d90878c5d4a7068369679cdf Signed-off-by: Mikko Perttunen &lt;mperttunen@nvidia.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Mark Zhang &lt;markz@gpartner-nvidia.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/414862 Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="414862" owner_email="markz@gpartner-nvidia.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/ch